# Line endings are pinned HERE, not by core.autocrlf.
#
# core.autocrlf=true is set on this machine, so git checks files out with CRLF and
# normalizes them back to LF on add. That round trip is invisible until something reads
# a source file as BYTES rather than as lines. Ours do: tests/nashville_js.test.mjs,
# tests/score_hands.test.mjs and tests/score_export.test.mjs slice arsenal/web/piano.js
# with src.indexOf("\n}\n"), which can never match \r\n}\r\n -- the slice comes back -1
# and the pin fails somewhere far from the cause.
#
# It bit twice in two days. On 2026-09-16 a fresh `git worktree add` checked out
# piano.js and the instrument modules with CRLF; on 2026-09-17 `git apply` of a
# worktree patch wrote CRLF into 17 files in the main tree. Each time the repair was a
# manual byte-level renormalization, which fixes the tree and not the trap.
#
# eol=lf makes the CHECKOUT side explicit, so every clone, every worktree and every
# `git apply` lands LF no matter what core.autocrlf says on the machine. The index was
# already LF for every pattern below (git ls-files --eol: 3883 i/lf, 0 i/crlf), so this
# file rewrites no blob -- it only stops the working tree from drifting away from it.

*.js     text eol=lf
*.mjs    text eol=lf
*.py     text eol=lf
*.md     text eol=lf
*.json   text eol=lf
*.html   text eol=lf
*.css    text eol=lf
*.frag   text eol=lf
*.vert   text eol=lf
*.yml    text eol=lf
*.toml   text eol=lf
*.txt    text eol=lf
# Added 2026-10-13: the house GLSL library and its cards are read as bytes too (the twin
# tests assert no \r in a module), and a fresh worktree checked all 102 .glsl and 73 .card
# files out with CRLF, failing 9 pins that passed in the main tree. Bash refuses CRLF.
*.glsl   text eol=lf
*.card   text eol=lf
*.sh     text eol=lf

# Windows batch is the deliberate exception: cmd.exe's parser expects CRLF, and LF-only
# batch files mis-parse around labels and GOTO. arsenal/tools/tiktok-ready.cmd is the
# live one. The blob stays LF in the repo; the checkout is CRLF, which is what runs.
*.cmd    text eol=crlf
*.bat    text eol=crlf

# Byte artifacts: never normalized, never diffed as text. (research/reviewed/
# origin-prelog-2026-04-11/.gitattributes marks *.aof and *.rdb for the same reason --
# autocrlf once stripped 18,435 CR bytes out of a RESP archive and made it unparseable.)
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.mid    binary
*.mp4    binary
*.woff2  binary

# =============================================================================================
# 2026-10-31, phase 3 of the modular-core detangle (Daniel approved the list 2026-10-30:
# "I love your proposed changes, fantastic work!!!").
#
# (a) THE REST OF THE TEXT SURFACE. The policy above is right and it stops short: MEASURED with
#     `git ls-files --eol` before writing this block, the INDEX is already LF for every tracked
#     file (0 i/crlf), while 164 tracked files still carry CRLF in a working tree -- and every one
#     of them is an extension the rules above do not name:
#         .jsonl 73   .ps1 31   .log 16   extensionless 11   .bat 8   .cmd 4   .gitignore 3
#         .tsv 3   .patch 2   .gitattributes 2   .ini 1   .out 1   .mdc 1   .svg 1   .spec 1
#     (.bat and .cmd are CRLF ON PURPOSE -- see the batch exception above; they are listed here as
#     evidence, not as a defect.) That residue is what produced the "CRLF will be replaced by LF"
#     warning on every commit and made out-of-tree diffs read as rewrites.
#
#     NO MASS REWRITE IN THIS BRANCH: these lines change nothing on disk today. They bind NEW and
#     RE-COMMITTED files to the index's LF, which is exactly the convergence the wish asked for.
#     TO RENORMALISE THE EXISTING TREE, as its own deliberate commit (never inside a feature
#     branch -- it touches 164 blobs and every open branch will conflict):
#         git add --renormalize .
#         git commit -m "renormalise: the line-ending policy, applied"
#     A no-op on a tree that is already converged, so it is safe to re-run as an audit.
*.jsonl  text eol=lf
*.ps1    text eol=lf
*.log    text eol=lf
*.tsv    text eol=lf
*.patch  text eol=lf
*.ini    text eol=lf
*.out    text eol=lf
*.spec   text eol=lf
*.mdc    text eol=lf
*.svg    text eol=lf

# The extensionless text files git's autocrlf was guessing about. Named explicitly so the answer
# is a rule rather than a machine setting: LICENSE/NOTICE/.gitignore/.gitattributes are TEXT and
# are byte-read by tests, so a CRLF checkout is a test failure waiting for a fresh clone.
LICENSE        text eol=lf
NOTICE         text eol=lf
.gitignore     text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf

# (b) docs/PHYSICS.md IS DERIVED -- scripts/generators/gen_physics_sheet.py regenerates it and
#     scripts/githooks/pre_commit.py stages the fresh copy on EVERY commit (`regenerate_derived`).
#     A merge of two derived sheets is meaningless: both sides are right and neither is the
#     truth, so git used to stop the merge with conflict markers in a file that the very next
#     commit would overwrite anyway. `merge=union` is a BUILT-IN git driver -- no per-clone
#     `git config merge.<name>.driver` is needed, which is why this is a driver rather than
#     "stop tracking it and regenerate": a fresh clone still gets the sheet, and a conflicted
#     merge still completes. The union result is TEMPORARY: it is a superset of both sides and
#     the next commit regenerates the one true sheet over it. Verified by hand on a scratch repo
#     before writing this line (merge produced both sides' lines, no markers, no manual resolve).
docs/PHYSICS.md merge=union
